messages.body_plain:
We've had this one on our TODO list for a long time. The short message is that we're thinking about it and working on it, but there's no timeline as to when it'll get into Stan.

The long: it's tricky. Under the hood, we're trying to minimize duplication of our code base. If we take the normal distribution as an example, we've written a single function that will allow each of the 3 parameters (y, mu, sigma) to be a scalar, an array, a vector, or a row vector. Each parameter can also be declared as data (C++ type double) or as a parameter (C++ type stan::agrad::var). Using template metaprogramming we have a function that will accept all 512 different type configurations. Inside the function, we're also efficiently calculating the log probability function by vectorizing and not calculating additive constants when they are functions of data and not parameters.

We have some ideas about how to extend it past a vector into matrices, but it's tricky. So far, we've been using arrays, vectors, and row vectors interchangeably.

Here's an example:

data {

  real y[3];

}

parameters {

  vector[3] mu;

  row_vector[3] sigma;

}

model {

  y ~ normal(mu, sigma);

}

So this currently works and it's the same as saying:
for (n in 1:3)
  y[n] ~ normal(mu[n], sigma[n]);

So when we start getting into vectorized matrixes, I think we may need to break backward compatibility for consistency, but I'm not sure. Let's change the example to:
data {
  matrix[3,3] y;

}
parameters {
  matrix[3,3] mu;
  matrix[3,3] sigma;
}

model {
  y ~ normal(mu, sigma);
}

I think we can all see that this should be doing:
for (i in 1:3)
  for (j in 1:3)
    y[i,j] ~ normal(mu[i,j], sigma[i,j]);

What if mu was defined as vector[3] and sigma was defined as row_vector[3]? Then the call should be equivalent to:
for (i in 1:3)
  for (j in 1:3)
    y[i,j] ~ normal(mu[i], sigma[j]);

So what should happen to mu if it was defined as "real mu[3]"? In this case it's ambiguous. Perhaps it shouldn't be allowed? Or should we define a convention?
If y were actually a 3x4 matrix, should we be clever and take an array to be in the direction that matches? If things have to match dimensions across 2+ directions, I think we may have to not allow mixed types for {array, vector, row_vector} to be consistent, but I'm not sure.

With regards to your example with all three arguments having the same dimension or a single matrix argument and scalars for the other arguments, it seems straightforward enough, but I think we want to really figure out how to tackle vectorization in any direction. Once we figure it out, I think it won't take too much time to get everything vectorized appropriately.

And this is loosely related to vectorizing multivariate distributions, which is also on our list.

Thanks for being patient. I'll try to look at it again after v2.0.0 is out.

Daniel

