>        matrix[3,3] sigma;
>     }
>     model {
>        for (row in 1:3) {
>          for (col in 1:3) {
>            X[row, col] ~ normal(mu[row,col], sigma[row,col]);
>            mu[row, col] ~ normal(0, 1);
>            sigma[row, col] ~ inv_gamma(6, 5);
>          }
>        }
>     }
>     |

You can rewrite the model here using one layer of vectorization:

|
model {
   for (row in 1:3) {

     X[row] ~ normal(mu[row], sigma[row]);
     mu[row] ~ normal(0, 1);
     sigma[row] ~ inv_gamma(6,5);
   }
}
|

If you just supply a row index to a matrix, it picks out that row.

If you're doing this, it's faster to represent the matrices as
arrays of vectors, which won't require copying and allocating (both
of which are cheap, by the way, compared to calling distribution
functions).

In general, the loops should go the other way (column then row)
for speed because that's how memory is organized for matrices.
For arrays of vectors, it goes the other way around.

These are all going to be fairly minor differences for matrices
this small, but the savings increase with the size of the matrix.

- Bob

